cts: C 100% reliable, quick correct decisions, positive crew atmosphere, keeps calm in crisis, but difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing further education. B: very conscientious, handles stress very well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, but makes nasty remarks, weak memory for numbers. Also B's pretentiousness? Actually \"makes nasty remarks about his colleagues\" and \"relatively weak memory for numbers\". Also earlier \"makes nasty remarks about his colleagues\" is a concern for crew cooperation. Also \"makes nasty remarks\" is a strong negative.\n\nWe need to weigh. The panel hasn't discussed A and D yet. But we need to compare C vs B. The question: Should we shift vote? We can argue that C's reliability and decision quality are paramount, and his calm in crisis indicates stress resilience comparable to B. Communication issues are concerning but may be mitigated; B's nasty remarks are more severe for crew cooperation. So we may keep C as leading.\n\nThus we can state we still recommend Candidate C, because reliability, decision quality, crew atmosphere, and calm under pressure outweigh B's stress handling and technical skills, especially given B's negative interpersonal behavior.\n\nProvide evidence: C 100% reliability, quick correct decisions, positive crew atmosphere, keeps calm in crisis. B's negative: makes nasty remarks, weak memory for numbers (attention). So we argue.\n\nThus vote remains Candidate C.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE: reference Agent 1 and 3, mention strengths and concerns, argue.\n\nNow METADATA_JSON with vote Candidate C.\n\nProceed."}
